            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Repeats each element of an array a number of times, in place.\n\nEach value is copied next to itself - 0, 1, 2 repeated twice is 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2 - not the whole array tiled end to end (that would be 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2; join copies with np.concatenate for that). Use it to stretch an array by a whole-number factor, to blow a small grid up into blocks (pixel doubling), or to hold each value for several steps.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.repeat <repeats: int> <axis: int>\nDefaults: repeats 2, axis 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.repeat 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Each array that arrives is repeated and sent; only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nrepeats (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many copies of each element. 1 passes the array through; 0 gives an empty array; a negative number prints a message and nothing is sent. Changing it does NOT send by itself - in the demo a metro re-sends the row ten times a second.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich axis to repeat along. On a 2-D array, 0 repeats each row and 1 each column. Set it to the number of dimensions the array has (1 for a row) and the node first ADDS a new axis there, so each value is copied into a new dimension: a row of 4 becomes 4 rows of copies (4 by repeats). A negative axis counts from the end (-1 is the last axis);\nanything past one-beyond-the-last is treated as the last axis.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narray out:\nThe repeated array: the chosen axis is repeats times longer.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA row of 4 values, 0 to 1, repeated 3 times along axis 0 gives 12 cells: each colour three times in a row. Raise repeats and the blocks widen (the numbers hide once cells get too narrow to hold them). Set axis to 1 and the same 4 values become a 4 by 3 grid - each row one value, copied across. np.shape shows the shape either way.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.concatenate joins whole arrays end to end - feed it the same array twice to tile it. np.stack piles arrays up along a new axis. np.reshape refolds an array without copying anything. t.repeat (on the t.roll page) is NOT the PyTorch version of this: it tiles the whole tensor.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 860,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Repeats each element of an array a number of times, in place.\n\nEach value is copied next to itself - 0, 1, 2 repeated twice is 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2 - not the whole array tiled end to end (that would be 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2; join copies with np.concatenate for that). Use it to stretch an array by a whole-number factor, to blow a small grid up into blocks (pixel doubling), or to hold each value for several steps.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.repeat <repeats: int> <axis: int>\nDefaults: repeats 2, axis 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.repeat 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Each array that arrives is repeated and sent; only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nrepeats (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many copies of each element. 1 passes the array through; 0 gives an empty array; a negative number prints a message and nothing is sent. Changing it does NOT send by itself - in the demo a metro re-sends the row ten times a second.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich axis to repeat along. On a 2-D array, 0 repeats each row and 1 each column. Set it to the number of dimensions the array has (1 for a row) and the node first ADDS a new axis there, so each value is copied into a new dimension: a row of 4 becomes 4 rows of copies (4 by repeats). A negative axis counts from the end (-1 is the last axis);\nanything past one-beyond-the-last is treated as the last axis.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narray out:\nThe repeated array: the chosen axis is repeats times longer.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA row of 4 values, 0 to 1, repeated 3 times along axis 0 gives 12 cells: each colour three times in a row. Raise repeats and the blocks widen (the numbers hide once cells get too narrow to hold them). Set axis to 1 and the same 4 values become a 4 by 3 grid - each row one value, copied across. np.shape shows the shape either way.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.concatenate joins whole arrays end to end - feed it the same array twice to tile it. np.stack piles arrays up along a new axis. np.reshape refolds an array without copying anything. t.repeat (on the t.roll page) is NOT the PyTorch version of this: it tiles the whole tensor."
        },
